      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Current geopolitical theory generally favors the argument that sanctions are highly ineffective in promoting democracy due to their tendency to strengthen authoritarian resilience and harm the very populations they aim to protect. Most academic consensus suggests that while powerful nations often weaponize them for transactional geopolitical leverage, the economic pain caused by these measures usually forces regimes to suppress dissent for stability rather than adopt democratic reforms. Ultimately, the weight of modern scholarship lies in the view that sanctions primarily reinforce the very coalitions they seek to dismantle without achieving long-term political liberalization in the target state.",
      "source": "target",
